# An example of vtium's configuration file. Copy it to /etc/vtium/vtium.conf
# and edit it. It is generated from crates/vtium-settings/src/keys.rs.
#
# The settings come first, one per line: key=value. For an on/off setting,
# write its key to turn it on and no-key to turn it off. Each setting is shown
# commented out, at its default. An option on vtium's command line overrides
# these lines, and a [look] or [head] section below overrides both.
#
# A comment may leave out details that docs/config.md gives for each setting.

# ══ Fonts and text ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The console's font and its size, who draws box-drawing and block characters,
# how blinking text looks, and which way text runs.

# ── Font ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## font-name: The console's font.
##
## The value is the name of a font set (a frit file, `NAME.vtfrit` in
## `/etc/vtium/fonts`). It can also be the name of a kbd console font, or with
## `font-engine = psf` the path of a PSF font; vtium then uses that one font.
##
## When unset, vtium uses the font from vconsole.conf. If there is none, or
## vtium cannot find the font, vtium uses its built-in font and logs why.
#
## font-frits: The directory that holds the font sets (the `NAME.vtfrit` files
## that `font-name` names).
##
## It must be an absolute path; vtium ignores a relative or empty one and logs
## why.
#
## font-paths: The folders where vtium looks, in order, for installed fonts
## that a font set's `font:` lines name.
##
## Separate the folders with `:`, as in `PATH`; each must be an absolute path.
## The list replaces the built-in list. vtium skips a folder that is not an
## absolute path, uses the rest, and logs why.
##
## When unset, vtium searches `/usr/share/fonts` and `/usr/local/share/fonts`.
## A user's own folder, such as `~/.local/share/fonts`, is searched only when
## the list names it, and the font helper cannot read it before someone logs
## in, because it then runs as `nobody`.
#
## font-engine: Which kind of font to use. When unset, vtium tries each kind
## the build supports, in the order listed.

#font-name=
#font-frits=/etc/vtium/fonts
#font-paths=
#font-engine=

# ── Font size and spacing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## font-size: The font size in points (1/72 inch).
##
## The size in pixels is points × DPI ÷ 72, at the display's density
## (`display-dpi`). It overrides a font set's `cell=` line. 0 picks a size to
## suit each display.
#
## font-hinting: How outline fonts are fitted to the pixel grid (hinting). It
## overrides a font set's `hinting=` line.
##
## `light` fits each character vertically only. `full` fits a TrueType font
## both ways, and treats a CFF font as `light`. Bitmap fonts are not hinted.
##
## This key has no effect yet: each font set's `hinting=` line decides, and
## `light` is used where it has none.
#
## font-line-spacing: Extra space between lines of text. It overrides a font
## set's `line-spacing=` line.
##
## The value is a whole percentage of the text height, rounded to whole pixels
## and split above and below each line, so the console has fewer lines. When
## `font-size` overrides a font set's `cell=` size, the character cell grows
## by the spacing. When unset, the font set's `line-spacing=` decides, and 0
## where it has none.

#font-size=0
#font-hinting=
#font-line-spacing=

# ── Box drawing and block characters ────────────────────────────────────────

## glyph-source: Who draws box-drawing, block and similar characters (six
## ranges): the font, or vtium itself.
##
## `font` uses the font's characters, `sprites` uses vtium's own drawings, and
## `auto` uses the font's where the font has the character. When unset, vtium
## uses the font for each range the font covers completely and its own
## drawings for the rest; with the built-in unifont, vtium always draws them
## itself.
#
## glyph-source-box: Overrides `glyph-source` for box drawing (U+2500 to
## U+257F).
#
## glyph-source-block: Overrides `glyph-source` for block elements (U+2580 to
## U+259F).
#
## glyph-source-braille: Overrides `glyph-source` for Braille (U+2800 to
## U+28FF).
#
## glyph-source-sextants: Overrides `glyph-source` for sextants (U+1FB00 to
## U+1FB3B).
#
## glyph-source-octants: Overrides `glyph-source` for octants (U+1CD00 to
## U+1CDE5).
#
## glyph-source-legacy: Overrides `glyph-source` for the other block shapes of
## Symbols for Legacy Computing.

#glyph-source=
#glyph-source-box=
#glyph-source-block=
#glyph-source-braille=
#glyph-source-sextants=
#glyph-source-octants=
#glyph-source-legacy=

# ── Missing characters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## What vtium shows for a character the font cannot display: `box` shows □
## (U+25A1), and `hex` shows the character's code in hex digits.

#missing=box

# ── Blinking ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## blink: Blink blinking text (SGR 5) and the cursor, every 500 ms. When off,
## neither blinks.
#
## blink-text: How blinking text (SGR 5 or 6) looks while a blink is off.
##
## `full` hides it, `partial` dims it halfway to its background, as faint text
## (SGR 2) is, and `off` keeps it steady. The cursor follows `blink` either
## way.
#
## blink-decorations: Blink the underline and strikethrough of blinking text
## with it.
##
## They hide while a blink is off with `blink-text = full`, and dim with
## `partial`. When off, they stay visible while the text blinks.

#blink
#blink-text=partial
#no-blink-decorations

# ── Font switching by programs ──────────────────────────────────────────────

## Let programs switch the console's font, and ask which font is in use
## (xterm's OSC 50).
##
## A program switches with `OSC 50 ; NAME ST` and asks with `OSC 50 ; ? ST`. A
## switch lasts until that program's session ends; then vtium returns to the
## font and size that `font-name` and `font-size` set.
##
## When off, vtium ignores OSC 50, queries included.

#allow-font-ops

# ── Right-to-left text ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Which way a paragraph of text runs when the program writing it does not
## say: `ltr`, left to right, `rtl`, right to left, or `auto`, which takes
## each paragraph's direction from its first letter.
##
## A paragraph is the text between two line ends. `ltr`, the default, starts
## each paragraph at the left edge, and `rtl` at the right edge. Under `auto`,
## a paragraph that starts with an Arabic or Hebrew letter runs right to left,
## and any other runs left to right.
##
## vtium reads this key when it starts, so a change takes effect when vtium
## restarts, and it never changes text already on the screen.
##
## A build without right-to-left support (the `bidi` feature) accepts this key
## and does nothing with it.

#text-direction=ltr

# ══ Colors ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The sixteen basic colors, the default text and background colors, and the
# colors of underlines and strikethrough.

# ── Palette ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## palette: The sixteen basic colors, and the default text and background
## colors.
##
## The value is the name of a palette, or `custom`, which takes the colors
## from the `palette-*` keys. Colors 16 to 255 are xterm's in every palette.
#
## palette-black: Color 0 (black) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-red: Color 1 (red) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-green: Color 2 (green) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-yellow: Color 3 (yellow) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-blue: Color 4 (blue) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-magenta: Color 5 (magenta) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-cyan: Color 6 (cyan) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-light-grey: Color 7 (light grey) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-dark-grey: Color 8 (dark grey) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-light-red: Color 9 (light red) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-light-green: Color 10 (light green) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-light-yellow: Color 11 (light yellow) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-light-blue: Color 12 (light blue) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-light-magenta: Color 13 (light magenta) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-light-cyan: Color 14 (light cyan) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-white: Color 15 (white) when `palette = custom`.
## palette-foreground: The default text color when `palette = custom`.
## palette-background: The default background color when `palette = custom`.

#palette=legacy
#palette-black=0,0,0
#palette-red=205,0,0
#palette-green=0,205,0
#palette-yellow=205,205,0
#palette-blue=0,0,238
#palette-magenta=205,0,205
#palette-cyan=0,205,205
#palette-light-grey=229,229,229
#palette-dark-grey=127,127,127
#palette-light-red=255,0,0
#palette-light-green=0,255,0
#palette-light-yellow=255,255,0
#palette-light-blue=92,92,255
#palette-light-magenta=255,0,255
#palette-light-cyan=0,255,255
#palette-white=255,255,255
#palette-foreground=229,229,229
#palette-background=0,0,0

# ── Underline and strikethrough colors ──────────────────────────────────────

## underline-color: The color of underlines (SGR 4 in every style, and SGR
## 21).
##
## `fg` uses the text's color, a palette number uses that palette color, and
## `#rrggbb` uses that color. A color a program sets with SGR 58 wins, unless
## `force-decoration-colors` is on.
#
## strike-color: The color of strikethrough (SGR 9). It takes the same values
## as `underline-color`.
#
## force-decoration-colors: Use `underline-color` even when a program sets its
## own underline color (SGR 58).

#underline-color=fg
#strike-color=fg
#no-force-decoration-colors

# ══ Keyboard and mouse ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The keyboard layout and key repeat, the mouse and its pointer, and copy and
# paste.

# ── Keyboard ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## xkb-model: The keyboard model, as xkeyboard-config names it.
## xkb-layout: The keyboard layout, such as `us` or `de`.
## xkb-variant: The keyboard layout's variant, such as `dvorak`.
#
## xkb-options: Keyboard options for xkb, separated by commas, such as
## `ctrl:nocaps`.
#
## xkb-keymap: A keymap file. When set, vtium uses it instead of the model,
## layout, variant and options, and falls back to them if the file cannot be
## used.
#
## xkb-compose-file: A compose table file, used instead of the locale's.
#
## xkb-repeat-delay: How long a held key waits before it starts to repeat. 0
## means 250 ms.
#
## xkb-repeat-rate: The time between the repeats of a held key. 0 means 50 ms.

#xkb-model=
#xkb-layout=
#xkb-variant=
#xkb-options=
#xkb-keymap=
#xkb-compose-file=
#xkb-repeat-delay=250
#xkb-repeat-rate=50

# ── Mouse pointer ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## mouse: Use mice, touchpads and touchscreens.
#
## natural-scrolling: Reverse the mouse wheel.
##
## The wheel then scrolls the other way, both in vtium's scrollback and for
## programs.
#
## soft-cursor: Draw the mouse pointer in software on every display, never
## with the display's hardware cursor.
#
## pointer-char: Show the mouse pointer as this character, drawn with the
## console's font.
##
## When unset, or when the font cannot display the character, the pointer is
## an arrow.
#
## pointer-hide-timeout: Hide the mouse pointer after this long without mouse
## activity, until it moves again. 0 never hides it.
#
## pointer-hide-while-typing: Hide the mouse pointer while you type, until it
## moves again. A key vtium handles itself, such as a shortcut, does not hide
## it.

#mouse
#no-natural-scrolling
#no-soft-cursor
#pointer-char=
#pointer-hide-timeout=20
#no-pointer-hide-while-typing

# ── Clipboard and paste ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## clipboard-write: Let programs copy text to vtium's clipboard (OSC 52). When
## off, vtium ignores what they send.
##
## vtium keeps the last text copied, by a program or by you, and the middle
## mouse button and the `key-paste` key paste it.
#
## key-paste: The key that pastes the copied text, as the middle mouse button
## does; it pastes once per press.
##
## With Shift, write the character Shift makes: `<Ctrl><Shift>V`, not
## `<Ctrl><Shift>v`. When empty, no key pastes.

#clipboard-write
#key-paste=<Shift>Insert

# ══ Key bindings ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The keys vtium acts on itself instead of sending them to the program. Each
# value lists key combinations, separated by commas, and an empty value hands
# the key to the program. The scroll keys and the prompt jumps are vtium's
# only on the main screen: on the alternate screen, where editors run, they
# reach the program. Ctrl+Alt+F1 to F12 always switch the VT.

# ── Scrolling and prompt jumps ──────────────────────────────────────────────

## key-scroll-up: Scroll the view back one line.
## key-scroll-down: Scroll the view forward one line.
## key-page-up: Scroll the view back one page. `Prior` is Page Up.
## key-page-down: Scroll the view forward one page. `Next` is Page Down.
#
## key-prompt-up: Scroll the view back to the shell prompt above, which OSC
## 133 marks.
#
## key-prompt-down: Scroll the view forward to the shell prompt below, or to
## the live screen.

#key-scroll-up=<Shift>Up
#key-scroll-down=<Shift>Down
#key-page-up=<Shift>Prior
#key-page-down=<Shift>Next
#key-prompt-up=<Ctrl><Shift>Up
#key-prompt-down=<Ctrl><Shift>Down

# ── Zoom, rotation and reset ────────────────────────────────────────────────

## key-zoom-in: Make the font of the console on screen larger.
## key-zoom-out: Make the font of the console on screen smaller.
#
## key-rotate-cw: Turn the head's displays a quarter turn clockwise. `Logo` is
## Super.
#
## key-rotate-ccw: Turn the head's displays a quarter turn counterclockwise.
#
## key-reset: Reset the console on screen, as the `reset` command does.
##
## It clears the screen and the scrollback, and turns off every mode a program
## set, the keyboard's too: use it when a program left the keyboard sending
## escape codes.

#key-zoom-in=<Ctrl>plus,<Ctrl>equal
#key-zoom-out=<Ctrl>minus
#key-rotate-cw=<Logo>plus,<Logo>equal
#key-rotate-ccw=<Logo>minus
#key-reset=<Ctrl><Shift>Delete

# ── Consoles and heads ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## key-console-next: Show the head's next console.
## key-console-previous: Show the head's previous console.
#
## key-console-open: Open a console after the one on screen, unless the policy
## is `closed`.
#
## key-console-close: Close the console on screen, unless it is the head's
## last or the policy is `closed`.
#
## key-focus-next: Make the next head active, from a keyboard that roams among
## the heads.
#
## key-focus-previous: Make the previous head active, from a keyboard that
## roams among the heads.

#key-console-next=<Ctrl><Logo>Right
#key-console-previous=<Ctrl><Logo>Left
#key-console-open=<Ctrl><Logo>Return
#key-console-close=<Ctrl><Logo>BackSpace
#key-focus-next=<Ctrl><Logo>Up
#key-focus-previous=<Ctrl><Logo>Down

# ══ Session and terminal ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The command each console runs, the terminal type it sees, and how much of
# its output vtium keeps.

# ── The console's command ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## login: The command each console runs, such as a login prompt or a shell.
##
## When unset, the command is `/bin/login -p` with `reset-env` on and
## `/bin/login` with it off. A word written `$NAME` is replaced by that
## environment variable's value, and `$$` writes a literal `$` at the start of
## a word.
#
## oneshot: Exit when the console's command exits, instead of starting it
## again.

#login=
#no-oneshot

# ── The console's terminal type ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## term: The value of `TERM` that programs on the console see.
##
## The default is vtium's own terminfo entry, `xterm-vtium`, which is also
## named `vtium`. With either name, vtium answers capability queries
## (XTGETTCAP) from that entry. With `kmscon`, vtium answers from kmscon's
## entry. With any other value, vtium answers every query as unknown.
#
## reset-env: Start each console's command with only the environment variables
## vtium sets. When off, the command also gets vtium's own environment.
#
## backspace-delete: Backspace sends DEL (0x7f). When off, it sends BS (0x08).

#term=xterm-vtium
#reset-env
#backspace-delete

# ── Scrollback ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## sb-size: How many lines of scrollback each console keeps, up to
## `sb-size-cap`.
#
## sb-size-cap: The most lines `sb-size` can ask for.
##
## When unset, it depends on the machine's memory: 10,000 lines below 2 GiB,
## 30,000 from 2 GiB and 100,000 from 4 GiB.
#
## sb-memory: The most memory each console's scrollback uses. When it is full,
## the oldest lines are dropped, as they are past `sb-size`.
##
## The value is in bytes, or in KiB, MiB or GiB with `K`, `M` or `G` (such as
## `6M`), up to a quarter of the machine's memory (or up to the default when
## `/proc/meminfo` cannot be read).
##
## When unset, vtium sizes it to fit each console's size in characters, and
## sizes it again when that changes.

#sb-size=1000
#sb-size-cap=
#sb-memory=5242880
# (in a build with images: sb-memory=4653056)

# ── Bell ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Ring the console's bell when a program sends BEL.
##
## A program can silence its own bells by resetting mode 1042, which starts
## set. When off, no bell rings.

#no-bell

# ── Synchronized updates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## The longest vtium waits for a program to finish a synchronized update (mode
## 2026) before it shows the screen as it is. 0 turns mode 2026 off.

#sync-timeout=200

# ══ Consoles and the VT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# How many consoles the heads can have, and the VT vtium runs on.

# ── Consoles ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## head-consoles-max: The most consoles one head can have. A head's `consoles`
## cannot open more than this.
#
## consoles-max: The most consoles all heads can have together.
##
## Every head keeps its first console. More consoles open, in the order the
## heads appear in vtium.conf, until the total reaches this number.

#head-consoles-max=4
#consoles-max=8

# ── The VT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## vt: The VT to run on: a number N for `/dev/ttyN`, a name under `/dev`, or a
## path. When unset, vtium runs on the VT it was started on, or else the next
## free one.
#
## switchvt: Switch to vtium's VT when vtium starts. When off, vtium waits
## until something else switches to it.

#vt=
#switchvt

# ══ On-screen display and notices ═══════════════════════════════════════════
#
# What vtium shows on top of the console to say which head and console you are
# looking at, and to report a problem, and their colors.

# ── On-screen display ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## osd: What the on-screen display shows at the top of each screen after a
## change, such as a console switch.
##
## `minimal` shows a dot in the top-right character cell: filled on the active
## head, hollow on the others. `full` shows a line across the top, such as
## `● desk · console 2 of 3`. `off` shows nothing.
##
## A notice about a problem appears whatever `osd` says.
#
## osd-timeout: How long the on-screen display stays visible after a change.
## `never` keeps it visible all the time.
#
## focus-mark: The old name for `osd` and `osd-timeout`.
##
## vtium reads it only when `osd` and `osd-timeout` are not set: `auto` means
## `osd = minimal`, `always` means `osd = minimal` with `osd-timeout = never`,
## and `off` means `osd = off`. When it is set, vtium logs the new keys.

#osd=minimal
#osd-timeout=3
#focus-mark=

# ── Notice colors ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## notice-foreground: The text color of a notice. A notice is a box that
## reports a problem, such as errors in vtium.conf. It appears whatever `osd`
## says.
#
## notice-background: The background color of a notice. While a notice is
## showing, the on-screen display's line appears in the same box, in the
## notice's colors.
#
## notice-border: The border color of a notice.

#notice-foreground=31,41,51
#notice-background=254,226,226
#notice-border=185,28,28

# ── On-screen display colors ────────────────────────────────────────────────

## osd-foreground: The text color of the on-screen display: the line of
## `osd = full` and the dot of `osd = minimal`.
#
## osd-background: The background color of the on-screen display: behind the
## line of `osd = full`, and in the character cell behind the dot of
## `osd = minimal`.
#
## osd-border: The border color of the on-screen display with `osd = full`.
## The dot of `osd = minimal` has no border.

#osd-foreground=229,231,235
#osd-background=31,41,51
#osd-border=97,110,124

# ── Other color schemes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Alternatives for the six colors above. To use one, remove the # at the
## start of its six lines. vtium uses whatever colors you set; it does not
## check that text stands out against its box.

## light: For a light terminal background: the on-screen display is a pale
## grey box with dark text and a grey edge, and a notice a dark red box with
## white text and a darker edge, so a problem differs from a status in
## brightness as well as in hue.

#notice-foreground=255,255,255
#notice-background=153,27,27
#notice-border=69,10,10
#osd-foreground=31,41,51
#osd-background=243,244,246
#osd-border=156,163,175

## dark: Both boxes are dark, for a dark terminal background: a notice is deep
## red with pale text and a light red edge, and the on-screen display near
## black with light grey text and a grey edge.

#notice-foreground=254,226,226
#notice-background=185,28,28
#notice-border=248,113,113
#osd-foreground=209,213,219
#osd-background=17,24,39
#osd-border=75,85,99

## color-blind-safe: For color blindness, a problem differs from a status in
## brightness and in an orange and blue pair, never in red against green: a
## notice is a bright orange box with near-black text, and the on-screen
## display a dark blue box with near-white text.

#notice-foreground=28,25,23
#notice-background=255,176,0
#notice-border=254,97,0
#osd-foreground=238,242,255
#osd-background=11,42,91
#osd-border=100,143,255

# ══ Displays ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Each display's resolution, colors and density, how long vtium waits for a
# display when it starts, and how it draws to them.

# ── Display resolution ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## use-original-mode: Keep each display's current resolution. When off, vtium
## sets each display's preferred resolution.
#
## mode: A display's resolution and refresh rate.
##
## `WxH[@RATE]` sets it for every display, `[CONNECTOR]WxH` for one connector,
## and `GPU[CONNECTOR]WxH` for one connector on one graphics card, named by
## its udev `ID_PATH`. Write one `mode` line per display; the most specific
## match wins. When unset, each display uses its preferred resolution. Setting
## it turns `use-original-mode` off.

#no-use-original-mode
#mode=

# ── Display colors and density ──────────────────────────────────────────────

## display-depth: How many colors or shades each display can show.
##
## `1` is black and white: each character cell is lit or dark by its colors,
## never by their brightness. `gray4` and `gray16` are for gray panels of 4 or
## 16 shades; vtium sends color and the panel converts it. `color` is full
## color. `auto` uses what the display reports.
#
## display-dpi: The pixel density (DPI) used to turn `font-size` and a font
## set's `size=` from points into pixels, instead of the density each display
## reports.
##
## When unset, vtium measures each display's density from the physical size it
## reports. If a display reports none, vtium assumes 96 DPI and logs a warning
## once for that display.

#display-depth=auto
#display-dpi=

# ── Waiting for a display ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## How long vtium waits for a display when it starts, counted from when its VT
## is shown.
##
## If no display appears in that time, vtium exits with status 1, so the
## unit's `OnFailure=` can start a getty. When vtium.conf's policy is closed,
## vtium waits as long as it takes, as with `forever`.
##
## vtium counts only while the VT is its own. If another program takes the VT
## before vtium's first frame is on screen, vtium waits for the VT to come
## back and then starts the full wait again.
##
## With 0, vtium does not wait: only a display that is ready when the VT is
## shown counts. `forever` waits as long as it takes.
##
## Once a display has appeared, vtium never exits for lack of one.

#display-wait=10

# ── Display buffers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## How many frame buffers each display keeps (DRM displays only; every display
## gets the same number).
##
## `3` is triple buffering: no frame waits for the previous one to reach the
## screen, and each display needs one more frame of memory. `2` is double
## buffering. `1` is single buffering, which uses the least memory, but vtium
## then draws into the buffer on screen, so a half-drawn frame can show.
##
## A framebuffer device (fbdev) always keeps one buffer. When unset, the build
## decides: the number it was built with (`VTIUM_DISPLAY_BUFFERS`), or else 3
## in a build with optional features, 2 in the smallest build (minspec), and 1
## only in a build smaller than that.

#display-buffers=

# ── Drawing with the GPU ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Draw with the GPU, in a build that supports it.
##
## It is off unless vtium was built with it on (`VTIUM_HWACCEL`).

#no-hwaccel

# ── Frame timer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Measure each display's frame times once a second, and write the figures to
## the log, show them on screen, or both.

#render-timer=none

# ══ Power and blanking ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# When the displays turn off, and the keys that reboot, power off or suspend
# the machine.

# ── Blanking the displays ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## dpms-timeout: Turn the displays off after this long with no key press or
## mouse action (a move, a click or the wheel). 0 never turns them off.
#
## program-blank: Let a program set the blanking timeout and wake the
## displays, as `setterm --blank` does on the Linux console.
##
## A program sets the timeout with `CSI 9 ; n ]` (n minutes, up to 60; 0 never
## blanks) and wakes the displays with `CSI 13 ]`. A terminal reset (RIS)
## returns the timeout to `dpms-timeout`. When off, vtium ignores these
## sequences.

#dpms-timeout=600
#program-blank

# ── Power keys ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## key-reboot: The key that reboots the machine.
##
## When unset, no key reboots.
#
## key-power-off: The key that powers the machine off.
##
## When unset, no key powers off.
#
## key-suspend: The key that suspends the machine to RAM.
##
## vtium asks logind to suspend. When logind is not running, or vtium was
## built without `logind`, vtium writes `mem` to `/sys/power/state`. When
## unset, no key suspends.

#key-reboot=
#key-power-off=
#key-suspend=

# ══ Images ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Sixel and kitty graphics, how much memory images use, which protocols vtium
# offers, and the helper that reads and decodes them.

# ── Image memory and protocols ──────────────────────────────────────────────

## image-storage-limit: The most memory each console uses for images, on both
## its screens together.
##
## The value is in bytes, or in KiB, MiB or GiB with `K`, `M` or `G` (such as
## `64M`), up to 1 TiB. `auto` gives each console enough for four screenfuls
## of its head's displays, from 16 MiB to 192 MiB. `0` turns sixel and kitty
## graphics off.
##
## A build without image support accepts this key and shows no images.
#
## sixel: Show sixel images.
##
## When off, vtium neither offers nor shows sixel: its device attributes (DA1)
## leave out attribute 4, and it ignores sixel data. vtium offers sixel only
## while `image-storage-limit` is above 0 and every display of the console's
## head can show images. A build without image support never offers it.
#
## kitty-graphics: Show images sent with the kitty graphics protocol.
##
## When off, vtium neither offers nor shows them: it ignores graphics commands
## and does not answer queries. vtium offers it only while
## `image-storage-limit` is above 0 and every display of the console's head
## can show images. A build without image support never offers it.
#
## max-graphic-size: The largest image a program can send, in pixels (`WxH`),
## for sixel and kitty graphics alike. vtium refuses a larger one.
##
## A build without image support accepts this key and shows no images.

#image-storage-limit=auto
#sixel
#kitty-graphics
#max-graphic-size=10000x10000

# ── Sixel colors ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## num-color-registers: How many colors a sixel image can use (its color
## registers), and the most a program can ask for with XTSMGRAPHICS.
##
## A build without image support accepts this key and shows no images.
#
## private-color-registers: Give each sixel image its own colors (mode 1070,
## after a reset).
##
## When on, each sixel image starts from the default colors. When off, images
## share their colors. A build without image support accepts this key and
## shows no images.

#num-color-registers=1024
#private-color-registers

# ── Image helper ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## The image helper: the program vtium starts, as the logged-in user, to read
## image files and shared memory for kitty graphics and to decode PNG and
## zlib-compressed images. vtium runs as root and does neither itself.
##
## vtium opens the program once, when it starts, and runs every helper from
## that file, so a newly installed helper takes effect when vtium restarts.
##
## vtium refuses a helper whose path is not absolute, that is not a regular
## file, that is setuid or setgid, that anyone but root or vtium's own account
## owns, or that its group or other users can write. It then logs why and
## refuses images: `EBADF` for files and shared memory, `EINVAL` for PNG and
## zlib. vtium starts the helper through `/proc/self/fd`, so `/proc` must be
## mounted.
##
## A build without image support accepts this key and starts no helper.

#image-helper=/usr/bin/vtium-image

# ══ Running vtium ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Where vtium finds its configuration, its log, the font helper that draws a
# font's characters, and the character cache.

# ── Debug log ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Log debug messages too, to help find what went wrong. When off, vtium logs
## information messages, warnings and errors.

#no-debug

# ── Font helper ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## The font helper: the program vtium starts to draw a font set's characters.
## It runs as `nobody` when vtium runs as root.
##
## vtium opens the program once, when it starts, and runs every helper from
## that file, so a newly installed helper takes effect when vtium restarts.
##
## vtium refuses a helper whose path is not absolute, that is not a regular
## file, that is setuid or setgid, that anyone but root or vtium's own account
## owns, or that its group or other users can write. It then logs why and uses
## its built-in font. vtium starts the helper through `/proc/self/fd`, so
## `/proc` must be mounted.

#font-helper=/usr/bin/vtium-config

# ── Character cache ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## How much memory vtium uses to cache drawn characters. When unset, `minspec`
## (small), or `performance` in a build with the `performance-cache` feature.

#glyph-cache=

# ══ Looks, heads and rules ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The sections after the settings. A look is a font and its size; a head is a
# screen with its own consoles; the rules send each display and each input
# device to a head. These are the sections vtium uses, as shipped, when
# vtium.conf has none.

# vtium.conf's sections: its looks, its heads, and the rules that send each
# display and each input device to a head.
#
# The settings come first, above the first section heading: vtium's own keys,
# which every head uses unless it sets its own, and the two keys of the
# sections, version and policy, in any order. When vtium.conf has no section,
# vtium uses the sections below, built into it from this same text, so a file
# written from them changes nothing until you edit it.
#
# vtium reads vtium.conf only when root owns it and neither its group nor
# other users can write it or a directory above it. vtium never changes the
# file. It logs each error with its line, and ignores what the error takes: a
# bad rule ignores that rule, an error in a head's section ignores that head
# and every rule that names it, an error in a look's section ignores that
# look, and an error in the structure of the sections, such as a missing
# version, ignores everything but the policy. An error here never makes vtium
# ignore a setting above the first section. vtium check-config FILE checks a
# file before you install it.
#
# A file with a section needs version = 1. A # starts a comment at the start
# of a line or after a blank; a # inside a word, as in --x#1, is part of the
# word.
version = 1

# The policy decides what the rules leave unsaid. With open, an input device
# no rule places roams, typing into the active head; no head is isolated
# unless it says so; and the keyboard safeguards, anti-lockout and the rescue
# key, are on. With closed, an input device no rule places is ignored; every
# head is isolated unless it says otherwise; and the keyboard safeguards are
# off, so no keyboard reaches a head the file does not send it to. With either
# policy, when the rules leave every display off, vtium turns on the boot
# display: with open for the first head, with closed only to show a notice.
# policy = open

# A look is a font and its size, which a head uses. A [look NAME] section
# takes font-name, which names its font set (frit); the font settings above;
# vtlook; and cell, the character cell in pixels, written as a font set's
# cell= line writes it. In one look, font-size overrides cell. The built-in
# configuration has no look, so its head uses the settings above. For example:
#   [look big]
#   font-name = desktop
#   font-size = 14
#
# A head is a screen: one or more displays that show the same consoles, with
# the input devices sent to it, a look and its consoles. A name has 1 to 64 of
# A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ + -, and roaming, ignore and off cannot be used. Each key
# below is shown at its default.
[head console]
# The look the head uses, the name of a [look NAME] section. When unset, the
# look is default: the [look default] section where there is one, else the
# settings above the first section.
# look = default
#
# How many consoles the head has, 1 to 50; it shows one at a time.
# consoles = 1
#
# The command the head's consoles run, written as vtium.conf's login takes it.
# When unset, the consoles run vtium.conf's login.
# login = /bin/login -p
#
# What the head's sessions do when it loses its last display: keep runs them
# on until a display of the head returns; hangup hangs them up at once; hangup
# DURATION (30, 30s, 5m or 1h) keeps them if a display returns within that
# time and hangs them up after it.
# when-display-lost = keep
#
# yes isolates the head: Ctrl-Alt-F<n> on its keyboards reaches its session as
# keys and switches no VT, the rescue key leaves its keyboards where they are,
# and it is never the active head, so no roaming input device reaches it. When
# unset, it is no with policy = open and yes with policy = closed.
# isolated = no

[rules]
# Each display and each input device goes through these rules on its own, top
# to bottom, and the first rule that matches it sends it:
#   display MATCH -> TARGET
#   input MATCH -> TARGET
# MATCH is * (every device of that kind) or one or more key:value matches, all
# of which must hold. TARGET is a head's name; for an input device, roaming
# (it types into the active head) or ignore (vtium never opens it, except that
# with the open policy it watches a keyboard for the rescue key); for a
# display, off (vtium turns it off). A display no rule matches stays off, and
# vtium logs a rule line to paste for it; an input device no rule matches
# roams with the open policy and is ignored with the closed one. vtiumctl
# detect lists every device with its keys filled in.
#
# Keys for an input device:
#   path:ID_PATH                  the USB port, and every interface below it
#   serial:VENDOR:PRODUCT:SERIAL  one unit, wherever it is connected
#   bluetooth:ADDRESS             one device, once bonded
#   id:VENDOR:PRODUCT             every unit of a model; devices lie
#   name:GLOB                     any device that gives itself that name
#   class:CLASS                   keyboard, key, mouse, touchpad,
#                                 touchscreen, trackball, pointingstick,
#                                 tablet, tablet-pad, joystick,
#                                 accelerometer or switch, as udev tags it
#   udev:NAME=GLOB                any udev property of the device
# For a head whose sessions need protecting, use path plus serial where the
# device has a serial, and path alone otherwise. id or name alone is a
# convenience, not a protection. In a glob, * matches any run of characters
# and ? one character; a value has no blank, so ? stands for a space in a
# name.
#
# Keys for a display:
#   edid:MAKER:PRODUCT:SERIAL     one monitor, the recommended key
#   connector-path:PATH           one port of a DisplayPort multi-stream hub,
#                                 globs allowed
#   connector:CARD/CONNECTOR      a port of a graphics card, globs allowed
#   card:GLOB                     every output of a graphics card
# A card is its udev ID_PATH (pci-0000:03:00.0), never cardN. A multi-stream
# connector's name, such as DP-8, can change after a replug or a resume; its
# PATH (mst:...) stays the same, so use connector-path there and connector
# only where the name is stable. The first number of the PATH, the card
# connector the hub is connected to, can change after a kernel or firmware
# update.
#
# path, serial, card, connector and udev read udev's database, and match
# nothing on a system that does not run udev.
#
# To customize, add rules above the built-in ones, such as:
#   display edid:AAA:0x0001:LARGE00001 -> desk
#   input path:pci-0000:15:00.0-usb-0:3.3 -> desk
#   input udev:VTIUM_IGNORE=1 -> ignore
# A udev rule copied into /etc/udev/rules.d/ can mark a device for the last of
# these (the examples are in /usr/share/doc/vtium/examples/):
#   SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{name}=="Headset", ENV{VTIUM_IGNORE}="1"
# and an hwdb entry copied into /etc/udev/hwdb.d/ can mark a security token or
# a numpad, which anti-lockout then does not count as a keyboard:
#   evdev:input:b0003vBBBBp0001*
#    VTIUM_ROLE=token
#
# The built-in rules: one head showing the same consoles on every display,
# with every keyboard, key device and pointing device typing into it, and
# every other input device ignored.
display * -> console
input class:keyboard -> roaming
input class:mouse -> roaming
input class:touchpad -> roaming
input class:touchscreen -> roaming
input class:trackball -> roaming
input class:pointingstick -> roaming
input class:key -> roaming
input * -> ignore
